        <section id="social-and-communication-disorders"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Social and Communication Disorders</h2>
              <p>Let’s look at three social and communication disorders, what they mean and how they relate to each other. Scroll through the slides below to learn more.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Autism spectrum disorder
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>'Autism spectrum disorder’ is a lifelong developmental condition, relative to brain development and impacts how a person perceives others, causing problems with communication and social interaction. It can also include limited and repetitive patterns of behaviour.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/demo/ua_u1_s1_p3_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Asperger syndrome
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Those with ‘Asperger syndrome’ don't have the learning disabilities that many autistic people have, but may have specific learning difficulties with understanding and processing language. Some with Asperger syndrome say the world feels overwhelming and this can cause considerable anxiety especially relating to other people, and taking part in everyday family, school, work and social life.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/demo/ua_u1_s1_p3_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          High functioning autism
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>'High-functioning’ autism isn’t an official medical term but its seen as more informative. It describes a milder form of autism or level 1 on the autism spectrum. Those with Asperger syndrome are often described as having high functioning autism showing symptoms but with little need for support and mostly living independent lives.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/demo/ua_u1_s1_p3_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
